A||N||6||Bob Englert||Fan switch on V6?||||5||
Z||000000||Bob Englert||02-01-2001||07:56 PM||||Where is the fan switch on the V6?  I have a 87 GT and I discovered that the fan switch does not come on when the temp climbs at idle.  Was working on the car with it idling and found the temp needle quite high.  The fan comes on with the A/C (but the A/C doesn't work) so I know the fan itself is OK. <P>Just need to know where the temp switch is on the block.  If anyone has a p/n that would be helpful.<P>Thanks!<P><BR>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 02-01-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||daved||02-01-2001||08:46 PM||||Someone posted this GM number for the switch:<P>3040 674	-	-	coolant fan switch (V-6 models and 84-86 4 cyl. Models)<P>I dont know for sure where it is located, probably at the engines front, beneath the EGR solenoid by the t'stat cap. It has a single green/white wire to the connector.<BR>There is a relay that is energised by the switch closure, so you should look at that as well. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||02-01-2001||10:35 PM||||Get the low temp fan switch from Rodney Dickman. The stock unit doesn't come on til some ridiculous temp of 235*F or so.||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||02-02-2001||12:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by daved:<BR><B>...I don't know for sure where it is located, probably at the engine's front, beneath the EGR solenoid by the t'stat cap. It has a single green/white wire to the connector...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>daved is correct - the switch has a single green wire w/white stripe.  The switch sits standing straight up - it screws into the top of the intake manifold, on the passenger-side-end of the engine.  To remove the wire, I believe you have to squeeze the sides of the its connector and pull straight up. <P>||||reg||14
Z||000004||Standard||02-02-2001||12:57 PM||||It could also be a bad relay, in the front compartment in front of the drivers side headlight.  Should be 3 or 4 wires going into a little black box.  Don't remember exact details..  The fan in my 86 GT wouldn't come on, thought it was the fan switch so I hooked up a manual one.  That didn't work, either.  Took out the relay, opened it up and cleaned it out, lots of crud on the contacts.  Not really supposed to open it up, it's sealed with some type of glue, but it's been working fine for the last 5-6 months.  Fan comes on with the engine compartment switch now.||||reg||
Z||000005||rogergarrison||02-02-2001||06:03 PM||||yes has a grn/wht wire. looking from the rear of car its just to the left and a little farther back than the radiator fill cap/upper rad hose.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Robert||02-02-2001||08:15 PM||||If you ground out that green wire going to the fan switch on the engine, and turn your key on, the fan should come on.  That’s the way my 87 GT was wired when I bought it.  <BR>If it does come on, then you have a bad switch, should cost around $20.00.  If the parts  shop only wants to charge you $10 for the switch, it’s the wrong one, (I learned the hard way).<P><P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://canterburytrails.com/photo/T2.GIF"> <P> “If you don’t look for that parking space near the entrance, you’ll never get it”||||reg||1
